```text
A public hearing for renewal of existing City liquor licenses (retail, restaurant, limited
retail, resort, microbrewery and bar and grill) filed with the City Clerk’s Office.  City Clerk
Carol Intlekofer noted for the record that 3 restaurant liquor licensees did not file for renewal
and advised per State Statute requirements, Shadows Brewing Co., LLC, Microbrewery Permit
application, has not yet been certified as complete by the Wyoming Liquor Division as the
statutory minimum malt beverage production quantity for the current licensing term had not been
met. She stated she had been advised that Shadow Brewing Co. intends to meet the production
requirement by March 31, 2009, therefore, meeting renewal requirements.  She further advised a
request to approve the renewal application contingent upon notification from the Liquor Division
that the application has been certified as complete will be made at the upcoming Finance
Committee meeting when renewal applications will be reviewed for recommendation. Bryan
Grzegorczyk, owner of Alf’s Pub and Package Liquor, and representative for Airport Golf
Course, thanked governing body members for the opportunity to hold a liquor license in
conjunction with his business within the City.  Upon inquiry, Chief Bob Fecht, Police Dept.,
reviewed compliance checks conducted during the current licensing year, stating the last checks
resulted in an 87% approval rate, which he believes is the highest in the state.  There being no
further comments or input and purpose of the public hearing having been met, Mayor Kaysen
closed the hearing and referred the applications to Finance Committee.
A public hearing for a restaurant liquor license application filed with the City Clerk’s
Office for Pizza Hut of Cheyenne, Inc. d/b/a Pizza Hut/Wing Street, 4503 Western Way,
Cheyenne, WY.  There being no comments or input and purpose of the public hearing having
been met, Mayor Kaysen closed the hearing and referred the application to Finance Committee.
“AN ORDINANCE AMENDING TITLE 17, ZONING, CHAPTER 17.08, ZONING
DISTRICTS ESTABLISHED AND SECTION 17.08.040 CHEYENNE CITY MAP OF THE
CHEYENNE CITY CODE 2002, BY CHANGING THE ZONING CLASSIFICATION FROM
CB COMMUNITY BUSINESS DISTRICT TO  HR-2 HIGH-DENSITY RESIDENTIAL-
DEVELOPING DISTRICT FOR LOT 6, BLOCK ONE, GATEWAY SOUTH, CHEYENNE,
WYOMING (LOCATED EAST OF WALTERSCHEID BOULEVARD AND SOUTH OF
WEST FOX FARM ROAD).”  Reporting for Public Services Committee, Mr. Brown moved to
approve on third and final reading, seconded by Dr. Rinne.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” – all
members of the governing body. (#3842)
“AN ORDINANCE CREATING SECTION 10.24.170, DISTRACTIVE DRIVING
PROHIBITED; EXCEPTIONS, OF CHAPTER 10.24, RULES OF THE ROAD GENERALLY,
OF TITLE 10, VEHICLES AND TRAFFIC, OF THE CODE OF THE CITY OF CHEYENNE,
WYOMING.”  John Fafoutakis and Ronald Nickle expressed concerns with how comprehensive
the ordinance will be and law enforcement concerns; types of driver distractions; legislating
citizens’ freedom and rights; message texting, and responsibility of drivers for alertness and safe
driving habits. Mr. Brown moved to approve on second reading, seconded by Mr. Collins.  Mr.
Pierson moved to amend by substitute ordinance, seconded by Mr. Collins.  It was advised the
substitute removes eating and drinking from the list of distraction types on page 1 and removes
law enforcement personnel from the exemptions.  Mr. Pierson moved to amend Section A, Item
5, of the substitute ordinance to add language after “Observing a television or video display” by
inserting OTHER THAN GPS NAVIGATION SYSTEMS”, seconded by Mr. Collins. Mr.
Fafoutakis suggested a further amendment to Section A to include non-alcoholic beverages.
Chief Bob Fecht, Police Dept., spoke in support of the amendment, stating the department has a
```

### Page 3 of 7

```text
policy relative to officers and operating in-car computers while driving, and explained offenses
cited under the ordinance would constitute a secondary offense, not primary offense, meaning
there must be other probable cause(s) or reason to pull a driver over.  Discussion included the
following topics:  GPS systems, secondary offense violation, and various types of driver
distractions.  Upon inquiry for the City Clerk’s record, it was clarified the proposed substitute
ordinance on the floor was the version dated Feb. 20, 2009 by the City Attorney staff.  Motion to
amend Section A, Item 5 of the substitute carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing
body. Upon inquiry regarding GPS navigation systems, Mr. Nickle discussed Bluetooth
communication technology relative to GPS. Discussion continued on other possible driver
distractions not set out within the ordinance, definition of distracted driver, and the phrase
“including, but not limited to, the following” in Section A.  Mr. Brown moved to further amend
the substitute by striking the word “computers” in Section A, Item #1, seconded by Ms. Broyles.
Upon inquiry, Chief Fecht advised since the installation of in-car cameras and computers,
officers’ operational accident rates has not changed.  Discussion followed on the purpose of the
proposed amendment, and distractions constituting a secondary offense.  Mr. Brown withdrew
his motion to amend with concurrence by Ms. Broyles as second to the motion.  Mayor Kaysen
clarified the motion to amend had been withdrawn.  Mr. Collins moved to further amend the
substitute by striking the phrase “including, but not limited to, the following:” and Items 1, 2, 3,
4 and 5 under Section A, seconded by Dr. Rinne.  Discussion on the amendment involved the
overall purpose of the ordinance, which is to avoid individuals from being distracted while
driving. Mr. Brown raised a point of clarification questioning whether the proposed amendment,
if approved, would also require an amendment to Section F, definition of terms.  Mr. Collins
modified his previous motion to amend, with concurrence by Dr. Rinne as second to the motion,
as follows: Strike the wording “including, but not limited to, the following:” and delete Items 1
through 5 in Section A, and remove Items 1, 2, 3, 5, and 6 in Section F.  Brian McNutt,  member
of Laramie County Amateur Radio Emergency Services and Amateur Radio Civil Emergency
Services (ham radio operators), expressed concerns the ordinance may pose regarding
emergency reporting (i.e. spotters reporting severe weather and assisting with community
emergency and disaster communications) provided by members to local public safety agencies
and the National Weather Service. He requested hand held radios be excluded from the
ordinance.  Upon inquiry, Chief Fecht reviewed evidentiary and prosecution issues involving law
enforcement if the ordinance were implemented.  Motion to amend carried.  Voting “yes” – all
members of the governing body.  Upon inquiry regarding the term “section” as used in Section D
(i.e. No motor vehicle shall be halted solely for a violation of this section), Bob Mason, Assistant
City Attorney, advised the word could be changed to “ordinance.”  Mr. Pierson moved to further
amend the substitute ordinance by changing the word “section” in Section D to “ordinance,”
seconded by Mr. Brown.  Motion to amend carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the
governing body. Ms. Ash moved to further amend the substitute by striking the phrase “As used
in this section, the following terms are defined:” under Section F and the number 4, leaving the
definition of Distracted Driver as the only wording under Section F, seconded by Dr. Rinne.
Upon inquiry, Assistant Attorney Mason suggested leaving the introductory phrase within
Section F for any future clarifications or definitions.  Ms. Ash modified her previous motion to
amend, with concurrence by Dr. Rinne as second to the motion, by leaving the introductory
phrase in Section F as it exists and renumbering Item 4, definition of distracted driver, to Item 1
under Section F.  Motion to amend carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body.
Motion to amend by substitute ordinance as amended (four amendments) carried. Voting “yes” –
all members of the governing body.  Main motion as amended by substitute (as amended)
carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body.
```

