ANNOUNCEMENTS/REPORTS/MOTIONS:
Consideration of proposal recommended by the Art In Public Places Committee to
acquire artwork to be known as “Iron Horse” from Lyle Nichols, Palisades, CO, for placement at
the Depot Plaza.  John Fafoutakis spoke in opposition to expending taxpayer funds toward public
artwork due to national economic conditions.  Mr. Valdez moved to approve in an amount not to
exceed $30,000.00, seconded by Mr. Collins.  Comment was made relative to a previous agenda
item involving re-appropriation of the budget and prioritizing. Upon inquiry regarding placement
of the proposed artwork in the Depot Plaza, John Goodier, Chairman, Arts in Public Places
Committee (AIPPC), advised the concept of the location was due to the sculpture’s theme
involving transportation, stating the artwork composition includes utilization of railroad
items/materials, and clarified the photo depiction that had been submitted with the agenda item
(on file with office of City Clerk) is not the final rendition of the sculpture and confirmed the
sculpture will provide for safe interaction with the public. He explained selection of the art piece
for recommendation; confirmed that no contract with the sculptor has yet been entered into;
stated the AIPPC considered the proposed sculpture as a “one of a kind type concept” and,
therefore, did not request proposals, and provided information on the history of AIPPC
ordinance, funding and budgetary earmarking.  Mr. Fafoutakis reiterated his previous position in
opposition to the proposed expenditure.  Discussion included creation of the AIPP program,
Committee role and program funding for artwork acquisitions.  Upon inquiry, Barb Dorr, City
Treasurer, explained the budget fund specific to the AIPP program, which is not a general
operating fund line item, and explained how transfers into the AIPP fund are made (i.e. budget
re-appropriation) following approval of the annual fiscal year budget.  Comments in support of
the proposal and the AIPP program were made.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of
the governing body with the exception of Dr. Rinne voting “no”.
